TI  - Anesthetic management of a neonate with type IV laryngotracheo-esophageal cleft.
PG  - 792-4
LID - 10.1111/j.1460-9592.2009.03067.x [doi]
AB  - Laryngotracheo-esophageal cleft is a rare congenital anomaly that occurs when the 
      trachea and esophagus fail to separate during fetal development. Clinical 
      severity varies greatly in anatomic cleft extent. We report the successful 
      management of the airway of a neonate with type IV laryngotracheo-esophageal 
      cleft for the gastric division surgery by dividing the esophagogastric continuity 
      between the esophageal orifice and the stomach using the balloon catheter and 
      remaining spontaneous breathing through the large cuffed tracheal tube inserted 
      into the esophageal orifice.
