TI  - Crisis resource management of the airway in a patient with Klippel-Feil syndrome, 
      congenital deafness, and aortic dissection.
PG  - 1220-5
LID - 10.1213/ane.0b013e3181957d9b [doi]
AB  - Klippel-Feil syndrome is a visually arresting deformity wherein severe 
      restriction of cervical motion predicts a difficult airway. Even minor 
      distraction of the neck risks cervical spine or neurologic injury, so regional 
      techniques, awake fiberoptic intubation, or awake tracheostomy are recommended 
      anesthetic approaches. We present a case of aortic dissection in a Klippel-Feil 
      syndrome patient for whom congenital bilateral deafness, coupled with the urgency 
      of the surgery, mitigated against the recommended first-choice techniques. Using 
      anesthesia crisis resource management methods, a multi-member team rehearsed 
      predefined roles and then managed the airway via inhaled induction of anesthesia, 
      followed by flexible fiberoptic intubation.
