IS  - 0014-2565 (Linking)
VI  - 208
IP  - 9
DP  - 2008 Oct
TI  - [False images of endobronchial tumor in CT-scan].
PG  - 444-6
AB  - Bronchoscopic identification of an endobronchial foreign body is an unexpected 
      finding in adults. Modern imaging techniques such as computerised tomography (CT) 
      may identify bronchial obstruction but not the cause. Moreover, images may be 
      suggestive of other pathologies, especially when the previous aspiration cases 
      are unknown. The most common CT findings in this setting are atelectasis, lung 
      hyperlucency, localised bronchiectases and lobar consolidation. CT diagnosis of 
      false endobronchial tumors in patients who have swallowed a foreign body is 
      rarely described in the bibliography. In view of the potential adverse outcome in 
      the case of wrong diagnosis we consider it is of interest to report two cases of 
      endobronchial tumors diagnosed by CT in which flexible bronchoscopy allowed 
      identification and extraction of an endobronchial foreign body.
