TI  - A case of tracheal injury with intubation during electroconvulsive therapy.
PG  - 67-9
LID - 10.1097/YCT.0b013e31816f75d5 [doi]
AB  - Respiratory complications related to electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) are a rare 
      occurrence. The need for endotracheal intubation during ECT is rarely indicated. 
      We report a case of a 47-year-old woman with severe gastroesophageal reflux 
      disease and depression who was intubated for her first 3 ECT treatments. She 
      developed a small tracheal tear after her third ECT treatment which resulted in 
      subcutaneous emphysema, pneumopericardium, and pneumomediastinum. The tracheal 
      tear resolved spontaneously and ultimately the patient underwent subsequent ECT 
      treatments successfully without intubation. This case is the first reported case 
      of complications related to endotracheal intubation during ECT.
