DP  - 2005 Sep-Nov
TI  - Specificity and sensitivity of clinical diagnosis for chronic pneumonia.
PG  - 1029-37
AB  - To compare clinical (non-invasive) diagnosis with bronchoscopic (invasive) 
      diagnosis, a total of 50 patients with chronic pneumonia (sputum smear-negative 
      for tuberculosis) were examined. Age range was 12-82 years. Sensitivity of 
      clinical diagnosis was 100% for tuberculosis and 81.8% for lung cancer; 
      specificity was 67.5% for tuberculosis and 89.7% for lung cancer. Clinical 
      diagnosis was correct in 43.4% of cases suspected of tuberculosis and 69.2% of 
      cases suspected of lung cancer. It may be reliable only for elderly women 
      smokers. Because tuberculosis is over-diagnosed in endemic areas, bronchoscopy is 
      strongly recommended for all cases of chronic pneumonia.
