DP  - 2006
TI  - Pneumoconiosis associated with an esophageal ulcer and uptake revealed in FDGPET.
PG  - 293-6
AB  - A 76-year-old man with a past history of pneumoconiosis visited the Department of 
      Gastroenterology in our hospital suffering from dysphagia. Gastroscopy revealed 
      an esophageal ulcer on the top of a torus lesion. Chest computed tomography (CT) 
      revealed it was caused by a swollen lymph node in the mediastinum. Squamous cell 
      carcinoma related antigen (SCC) was elevated to 1.8 ng/ml. To rule out 
      malignancy, we performed fluorine-18 deoxyglucose positron emission tomography 
      (FDGPET) which revealed a significantly increased uptake in a nodular lesion in 
      the right upper lobe and mediastinal lymph nodes. Biopsy and cytology of the 
      nodular lesion revealed only pneumoconiosis. We must be careful when we interpret 
      the findings of FDGPET in pneumoconiosis patients.
