TI  - Adult case of accessory cardiac bronchus presenting with bloody sputum.
PG  - 641-4
AB  - We report an adult case of accessory cardiac bronchus (ACB) which extended from 
      the carina to the diaphragm. A 32-year-old woman, with a history of frequent 
      respiratory infections since childhood, recently presented with bloody sputum, 
      and was admitted to our hospital. The ACB was detected as a supernumerary 
      bronchus diverging from tracheal bifurcation. Complete resection of the ACB was 
      performed by video-assisted thoracic surgery via minithoracotomy, approaching 
      from the 5th intercostal space. The bloody sputum was caused by chronic 
      inflammation of the ACB. She has been asymptomatic since surgery.
