TI  - Techniques for performing tracheostomy.
PG  - 488-96
AB  - One of the most commonly performed procedures in the critically ill, tracheostomy 
      has been described and used for lifesaving treatment of upper-airway obstruction 
      for at least 3,500 years. The procedure can be performed surgically in the 
      operating room or at the bedside in the intensive care unit. Recently, 
      percutaneous techniques performed by a variety of specialists have become popular 
      alternatives to open surgical tracheostomy. Various devices have been developed 
      to minimize identified risk and improve the simplicity of the procedure. These 
      techniques and devices are described in this paper.
