PMID- 15718404
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20050818
LR  - 20191210
IS  - 8750-7587 (Print)
IS  - 0161-7567 (Linking)
VI  - 98
IP  - 5
DP  - 2005 May
TI  - In vivo evaluation of the effectiveness of bronchial thermoplasty with computed 
      tomography.
PG  - 1603-6
AB  - A recent study has reported that the application of thermal energy delivered 
      through a bronchoscope (bronchial thermoplasty) impairs the ability of airway 
      smooth muscle to shorten in response to methacholine (MCh)(Danek CJ, Lombard CM, 
      Dungworth DL, Cox PG, Miller JD, Biggs MJ, Keast TM, Loomas BE, Wizeman WJ, Hogg 
      JC, and Leff AR. J Appl Physiol 97: 1946-1953, 2004). If such a technique is 
      successful, it has the potential to serve as a therapy to attenuate airway 
      narrowing in asthmatic subjects regardless of the initiating cause that 
      stimulates the smooth muscle. In the present study, we have applied 
      high-resolution computed tomography to accurately quantify the changes in airway 
      area before and after a standard MCh aerosol challenge in airways treated with 
      bronchial thermoplasty. We studied a total of 193 airways ranging from 2 to 15 mm 
      in six dogs. These were divided into treated and control populations. The MCh 
      dose-response curves in untreated airways and soon-to-be-treated airways were 
      superimposable. In contrast, the dose-response curves in treated airways were 
      shifted upward at all points, showing a significantly decreased sensitivity to 
      MCh at both 2 and 4 wk posttreatment. These results thus show that treated 
      airways have significantly increased luminal area at any dose of inhaled MCh 
      compared with untreated airways. The work in this study thus supports the 
      underlying concept that impairing the smooth muscle may be an effective treatment 
      for asthma.
