TI  - Intratracheal cocaine induced myocardial infarction: an unusual complication of 
      fibreoptic bronchoscopy.
PG  - 733-4
AB  - A 73 year old man developed chest pains 5 minutes after fibreoptic bronchoscopy. 
      The procedure had been performed without sedation following an intratracheal 
      injection of 5 ml 2.5% cocaine solution and xylocaine spray to the pharynx for 
      topical anaesthesia. A 12-lead electrocardiogram showed an evolving anterior 
      myocardial infarction. Cardiac catheterisation revealed coronary artery spasm in 
      the proximal left anterior descending artery at the site of non-significant 
      plaque disease. The risk factors, mechanisms, and treatment of cocaine induced 
      myocardial infarction following intratracheal injections are discussed.
