TI  - Diplopia as an initial manifestation of disseminated non-hodgkin's lymphoma.
PG  - 35-8
AB  - A 37-year-old male presented a sudden diplopia. Ocular examination showed a 
      partial paresis of the left eye (LE) on the left gaze, progressing in a few days 
      towards a total ophthalmoplegia. Further investigation revealed an intra-orbital 
      mass, immunohistologically diagnosed as a Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma (DLBCL), 
      according to the WHO classification. Since the patient was in an advanced, 
      disseminated stage of the disease (IVA-E), treatment was based on systemic and 
      intrathecal chemotherapy with a pancranial radiotherapy. The clinical course was 
      poor with only a 10-month survival. We wish to stress that the possibility of 
      orbital malignancy in young adults with acute onset of ophthalmoplegia should be 
      included in the differential diagnosis.
