TI  - Pulmonary care of children and adolescents with developmental disabilities.
PG  - 1299-314, viii
LID - 10.1016/j.pcl.2008.08.006 [doi]
AB  - Caring for children and adolescents with developmental disabilities is 
      challenging because of their susceptibility to varying degrees of respiratory 
      morbidity and mortality. This article discusses the common pulmonary problems 
      affecting children with developmental disabilities. Topics to be discussed 
      include gastroesophageal reflux, drooling, and dysphagia and their relation to 
      aspiration and aspiration pneumonia, upper airway obstruction and obstructive 
      sleep apnea, and the role of airway clearance. Case studies are used to introduce 
      a discussion of the underlying etiology, currently accepted methods to evaluate 
      the conditions, and evidence-based treatment options. The goal of managing these 
      problems is to use anticipatory guidance when possible and choose therapies that 
      improve the child's quality of life with minimal side effects.
