TI  - Corynebacterium ulcerans infection of the lung mimicking the histology of 
      Churg-Strauss syndrome.
PG  - 1237-9
AB  - We report the first case of pulmonary Corynebacterium ulcerans infection 
      mimicking Churg-Strauss syndrome (CSS). Productive cough, fever, general fatigue, 
      and weight loss developed in a 50-year-old man. Laboratory data revealed 
      prominent eosinophilia and elevated serum IgE. On chest images, multiple nodules 
      and cavities were predominantly detected in the right lung. Histopathologic 
      examination showed necrotizing granulomas and vasculitis with massive 
      eosinophilic infiltration identical to the findings seen in CSS; however, 
      clusters of Gram-positive, coryneform rods were observed in the alveolar spaces. 
      A toxigenic strain of C ulcerans was isolated from lung tissue. The patient was 
      treated with antibiotics, and a favorable clinical course ensued.
