VI  - 15
IP  - 3
DP  - 2005 Jun
TI  - Removal of an unexpected tracheal foreign body after five months.
PG  - 342-5
AB  - Foreign body aspiration can produce serious pulmonary diseases. Timely diagnosis 
      and appropriate treatment is important to prevent long-term complications in 
      affected children. We report the case of a 15-month-old child with a 5-month 
      history of regurgitation, vomiting, recurrent tracheobronchitis, and pneumonia. 
      The diagnosis was gastroesophageal reflux. The laryngotracheal endoscopy revealed 
      a rabbit vertebra partially obstructing the airway at the level of the cricoid 
      cartilage. With a rigid bronchoscope and forceps equipped with a telescope, it 
      was possible to disengage and extract the foreign body. Six months later 
      endoscopic control revealed no residual alterations in the larynx and trachea.
