IS  - 0886-4470 (Linking)
VI  - 130
IP  - 11
DP  - 2004 Nov
TI  - Tracheal anomalies in Pfeiffer syndrome.
PG  - 1298-302
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To determine the types and frequency of airway anomalies in patients 
      with Pfeiffer syndrome. DESIGN: Retrospective case series. SETTING: Academic 
      tertiary care pediatric hospital. PARTICIPANTS: Eleven patients with Pfeiffer 
      syndrome, 6 of whom were severely affected, were identified. All were included in 
      the study. MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: Presence of tracheal anomalies, need for 
      tracheotomy, and length of life. RESULTS: The 6 severely affected patients had 
      mutations in genes that code for fibroblast growth factor receptor 2 (S351C [3 
      patients]; C342S [2 patients]; and W290C [1 patient]). Five of these patients 
      were diagnosed during bronchoscopy or tracheotomy as having a congenital tracheal 
      cartilaginous sleeve. In 1 patient, supportive care was withdrawn at 2 weeks of 
      life, and the patient died. The remaining 5 patients required tracheotomy because 
      of severe upper airway obstruction. Three of these patients died (at ages 9 
      months and 7 and 15 years). Two are still alive at ages 23 and 18 months. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Patients with Pfeiffer syndrome manifest significant airway 
      pathologic conditions. Upper airway obstruction is related to midface hypoplasia 
      and secondary nasal obstruction. Tracheal anomalies have been infrequently 
      reported.
