DP  - 2004 Aug 7
TI  - [Massive haemoptysis: primary care, diagnosis and treatment].
PG  - 1582-6
AB  - Massive haemoptysis is life-threatening because of asphyxiation from flooding of 
      the central airways with blood. The first step in treatment includes airway 
      protection, usually managed by intubation. Imaging may be inconclusive, and 
      bronchoscopy--rigid or flexible--is essential to establish the site and cause of 
      the bleeding. Bronchoscopy may be therapeutic in that a balloon catheter may be 
      inserted in the bleeding airway in order to tamponade the source of bleeding. 
      Embolisation of pathological bronchial arterial vessels is the treatment of 
      choice in most cases; surgery is limited to causes that cannot be managed by the 
      endovascular treatment modality, or to cases that fail to respond to 
      embolisation.
