TI  - [Hemoptysis].
PG  - 975-9
AB  - Hemoptysis is a common respiratory symptom that always requires investigation. 
      Its severity varies from bloodstained sputum to life-threatening hemoptysis. 
      Blood originates most often from bronchial circulation. The most common causes of 
      hemoptysis are tuberculosis, lung carcinoma, bronchiectasis but idiopathic forms 
      are frequent. The diagnostic investigation is based mainly on history, chest 
      radiograph, fibroscopy and CT scan of the thorax. The goal is to determine the 
      cause as well as the site of bleeding. The management of life-threatening 
      hemoptysis is mainly based on either surgical approach or bronchial artery 
      embolization. The majority of centers favor this latter approach.
