VI  - 56
IP  - 4
DP  - 2001 Aug
TI  - Tracheal stenting for rupture of the posterior wall of the trachea following 
      percutaneous tracheostomy.
PG  - 320-1
AB  - Perforation of the posterior wall of the trachea during percutaneous tracheostomy 
      is a recognised complication. Treatment by either conservative or surgical 
      management has been described. We report two patients who developed posterior 
      tracheal wall perforation following percutaneous tracheostomy who were 
      successfully treated by insertion of covered, expandable metallic stents. This is 
      a well tolerated procedure that has minimal complications and can be performed at 
      the bedside. We recommend that tracheal stenting should be considered for the 
      management of posterior tracheal wall perforation following percutaneous 
      tracheostomy in those patients unsuitable for surgical intervention.
