VI  - 17
IP  - 3
DP  - 2000 Jun
TI  - [Chronic necrotizing pulmonary aspergillosis and ankylosing spondylarthritis].
PG  - 683-6
AB  - Fibro-bullous disease is a late complication of ankylosing spondylarthritis. Its 
      radiologic features frequently mimics tuberculosis or excavated neoplasm. About 
      50 to 65% of patients with ankylosing spondylarthritis have chronic aspergillus 
      colonization of their airways. Pulmonary aspergillosis infection occurs in 10 to 
      30% during evolution. We report two cases of patients with spondylarthritis who 
      developed chronic necrotizing pulmonary aspergillosis. Diagnosis was based on 
      serology, mycologic examination of expectoration and in one case on histology 
      obtain by transbronchial biopsies. Pulmonary complications due to aspergillus 
      species frequently reveal an associated fibro-bullous disease in patients with 
      ankylosing spondylarthritis. This can be reported to diminished chest wall 
      movements in such patients. Treatment is based on administration of anti-fungal 
      drugs. In case of medical treatment failure, thoracic surgery may be indicated.
