TI  - Tracheal lipoma: a rare intrathoracic neoplasm.
PG  - 497-500
AB  - Primary tracheal lipomas are extremely rare neoplasms. The typical patient is a 
      middle-aged man with complaints of cough and shortness of breath. Often, the 
      diagnosis is delayed, and patients are treated for asthma or bronchitis. The 
      diagnosis of a tracheal lipoma is best approached by computed tomography (CT) and 
      bronchofibroscopy. Tracheobronchial lipomas may be successfully excised 
      endoscopically or by laser therapy. Open surgical resection is required when the 
      lipoma extends extraluminally.
