VI  - 19
IP  - 4
DP  - 2006 Oct
TI  - [Primary lung tumors].
PG  - 223-7
AB  - Pulmonary neoplasia in children is usually due to methastatic disease because 
      primary lung tumors are very unfrequent. Due to its' rarity they are usually not 
      included in the differential diagnosis of lung masses, so treatment is delayed 
      and prognosis is worsened. Herein, we show our experience in the management of 
      five primary tumors of the lung or the airway: one tracheal, three bronchial, and 
      another intraparenchymatous. We study the clinical behaviour, diagnostic work-up, 
      treatment, histology, and follow-up. Despite its rarity, a diagnosis of pulmonary 
      tumor should be considered in any child with respiratory symptoms that does not 
      improve with standard therapy. An early and accurate diagnosis and an adequate 
      treatment are crutial in the prognosis of these patients.
