DP  - 2005 Dec
TI  - [A case of bronchial foreign body due to citrus fruit seed aspiration showing 
      multiple pulmonary infiltration repeatedly].
PG  - 746-50
AB  - We report a case of a bronchial foreign body in a 76-year-old citrus fruit 
      farmer. The patient was detected patchy infiltration (ground-glass attenuation) 
      of the right upper lung field on the chest X-ray on Dec. 26th, 2003. The shadow 
      tended to disappear after treatment with antibiotics. The same shadow was 
      detected again 10 months later and the patient underwent a bronchoscopic 
      examination. A foreign body was found lodged in the center of the right upper 
      bronchus, associated with bronchial stenosis due to mucosal edema. The abnormal 
      shadow disappeared after the foreign body, which we decided was a citrus fruit 
      seed, was removed. From the time course of the present illness and a 
      retrospective evaluation of previous chest X-rays, the patient had aspirated the 
      foreign body 18 months prior to his admission for bronchoscopy. We should be 
      careful of the possibility of foreign bodies even when the elderly do not present 
      a history of foreign body aspiration. It is important to consider the possibility 
      of a bronchial foreign body in patients with repeated pneumonia, and to perform 
      bronchoscopy aggressively.
