TI  - Laryngeal nerve monitoring during thyroid surgery in pregnancy.
PG  - 369-71
AB  - A 32-week parturient required partial thyroidectomy for suspicious carcinoma. The 
      surgeon requested laryngeal nerve monitoring to decrease the chances of laryngeal 
      nerve injury during surgery. After rapid-sequence induction of general anesthesia 
      and intubation, a size 3 laryngeal mask airway was inserted posterior to the 
      endotracheal tube and the cuff inflated with 15 mL of air. A fiberoptic 
      bronchoscope inserted through the laryngeal mask airway provided an unhindered 
      view of vocal cords for laryngeal nerve identification and testing during 
      surgery. This combined technique also offered the advantages of a secured airway, 
      as well as positive pressure ventilation in the parturient during thyroid 
      surgery.
