TI  - Flexible fiberoptic bronchoscopy through the laryngeal mask airway in a small, 
      premature neonate.
PG  - 268-71
AB  - The laryngeal mask airway (LMA) was introduced as a supraglottic device in 
      anesthesia for routine use in the normal adult and pediatric population. Because 
      the distal end of properly placed LMA faces the laryngeal inlet, this device can 
      be used as a guide to flexible fiberoptic bronchoscopy (FFB) performance. In this 
      clinical case, we present a small premature neonate with severe congenital 
      biphasic stridor. FFB was performed successfully through the LMA while 
      maintaining a patent airway during general anesthesia and permitting spontaneous 
      respiration, as well as allowing assisted ventilation when necessary. An 
      immediately subglottic intramural mass was revealed and tracheostomy was 
      performed.
