TI  - Screening for non-small cell lung cancer.
PG  - 253-8
AB  - Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer mortality and is usually discovered at 
      an advanced stage, when treatment is generally not effective. Many researchers 
      have investigated the value of screening for lung cancer, which would 
      theoretically allow earlier detection and more effective treatment. 
      Unfortunately, no trials of screening strategies for lung cancer have shown a 
      mortality benefit, and as a result, no major medical organization currently 
      recommends screening. Research continues to seek proof of the benefit of 
      screening as new techniques are developed, including low-dose spiral computed 
      tomography (CT), autofluorescence bronchoscopy, and advanced techniques of sputum 
      analysis. Although there are promising data on the sensitivity of these newer 
      screening methods, especially low-dose CT, for detecting early lung cancer, none 
      of the published trials are controlled, and they have not yet proven a decrease 
      in mortality. There are ongoing randomized, controlled trials aiming to 
      demonstrate a mortality benefit. Patients who are interested in being screened 
      for lung cancer should be encouraged to participate in well-designed clinical 
      trials whenever possible.
