DP  - 2004
TI  - Bronchoscopic diagnosis of ruptured pulmonary hydatid cyst presenting as 
      nonresolving pneumonia: report of two patients.
PG  - 363-8
AB  - The diagnosis and management of pulmonary hydatid disease represents an important 
      clinical problem in areas of the world endemic to echinococcal infection. We 
      report two patients, ages 14 and 34, respectively, who were admitted to our 
      clinic for investigation of nonresolving pneumonia. Neither had responded to 
      antibiotics prior to admission. Chest x-rays demonstrated lobar collapse and 
      consolidation in both patients. Fiberoptic bronchoscopy revealed laminated 
      membrane of hydatid cyst occluding the bronchus of apicoposterior and anterior 
      segmental bronchi of the left upper lobe in the first patient and the anterior 
      segment of the right upper lobe in the second patient. The diagnoses were 
      confirmed at the time of surgery.
