TI  - Laryngeal ultrasound to assess vocal fold paralysis in children.
PG  - 429-31
AB  - The purpose of this study was to assess the practicality and validity of 
      laryngeal ultrasound to establish vocal fold movement in children with suspected 
      vocal fold palsy. Fifty-five consecutive patients (age range three days to 12 
      years) with suspected vocal fold palsy underwent both laryngoscopy and laryngeal 
      ultrasound. Ultrasonographic findings correlated with endoscopic findings in 81.2 
      per cent of cases. This, however, rose to a concordance rate of 89.5 per cent in 
      patients aged over 12 months. Laryngeal ultrasound is well-tolerated, safe and 
      non-invasive and the authors feel that it is a useful adjunct to endoscopy in the 
      diagnosis of vocal fold palsy.
