DP  - 2003 Jul
TI  - Predictors of exercise oxygen desaturation following major lung resection.
PG  - 145-8
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To identify predictors of postoperative exercise oxygen desaturation 
      (EOD) in patients submitted to lobectomy or pneumonectomy for lung carcinoma. 
      PATIENTS AND METHODS: A consecutive series of 227 patients with non-small cell 
      lung cancer submitted to lobectomy or pneumonectomy from January 2000 through 
      October 2002 were prospectively analyzed. Maximal stair-climbing tests were 
      performed preoperatively (the day before the operation) and postoperatively (on 
      average, 9.2 days after operation) in room air for all patients. A fall in oxygen 
      saturation during the exercise below 90% was termed 'desaturation'. Univariate 
      and multivariate analyses were performed to identify predictors of postoperative 
      EOD. RESULTS: Thirty-five patients (15.4%) developed postoperative EOD. After 
      multivariate analysis, the only independent predictor of postoperative EOD 
      resulted a reduction in oxygen saturation during the preoperative exercise 
      (P=0.0004). CONCLUSIONS: Patients with a reduction in oxygen saturation during 
      the preoperative exercise test are at increased risk to develop a postoperative 
      EOD below 90%. A postoperative exercise test should be performed in all these 
      patients. Should EOD be confirmed, an intermittent home oxygen therapy is 
      recommended in order to facilitate recovery from operation and improve the 
      quality of life.
