DP  - 2003 Apr
TI  - Endobronchial post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder and its management 
      with photodynamic therapy: a case report.
PG  - 474-7
AB  - Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) is a recognized complication 
      of solid-organ transplantation. We describe a 56-year-old man with end-stage 
      idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis who underwent right-sided single-lung 
      transplantation and who had biopsy-proven PTLD involving the main bronchi 3 
      months later. The lesions recurred despite endobronchial electrosurgery and 
      systemic therapy with interferon for 3 months. Interferon was deceased and 
      anti-CD20 therapy and photodynamic therapy were performed concurrently using 
      flexible bronchoscopy. The subsequent follow-up endobronchial biopsy specimen 
      results were negative for PTLD. This case is the first reported use of 
      photodynamic therapy combined with anti-CD20 for treating PTLD. Endobronchial 
      PTLD is a rare complication of lung transplantation and can be managed 
      successfully with photodynamic therapy.
