PMID- 11989128
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20021204
LR  - 20061115
IS  - 0034-1193 (Print)
IS  - 0034-1193 (Linking)
VI  - 93
IP  - 4
DP  - 2002 Apr
TI  - [Assessment of anxiety levels in patients during diagnostic endoscopy].
PG  - 240-4
AB  - Patients with increased pre-endoscopic anxiety take advantage of the use of 
      conscious sedation. However, the habit of using premedication varies according to 
      the type of endoscopic examination. Aims of this study were: to evaluate whether 
      different endoscopic procedures may have different effects on patient anxiety; 
      and whether anxiety affects patient tolerance. 163 consecutive outpatients 
      undergoing endoscopy (75 gastroscopy, 51 colonoscopy and 37 bronchoscopy) were 
      interviewed to evaluate pre-endoscopy anxiety, by using the Spielberger 
      State-Trait Anxiety Inventory. After endoscopy, endoscopists rated patient 
      cooperation on a 10-cm visual analogue scale. Females had state and trait anxiety 
      levels higher than males (50.49 +/- 1.37 vs 45.07 +/- 1.20, p < 0.01; and 44.46 
      +/- 1.06 vs 38.48 +/- 1.01, p < 0.01). Anxiety levels were not related with the 
      type of endoscopic procedure. A direct relationship was observed between state 
      and trait anxiety (p < 0.001). Patient cooperation to endoscopy was inversely 
      related with both trait (p < 0.05) and state anxiety (p < 0.01). In conclusion, 
      gastroscopy, colonoscopy and bronchoscopy seem to induce similar pre-endoscopy 
      anxiety levels. Therefore, the different frequency in the use of intravenous 
      sedation between bronchial and gastrointestinal endoscopy does not seem 
      justified.
