TI  - Reinforcement of an end-to-end tracheal resection anastomosis with fibrin glue: a 
      case report.
PG  - 234-6, 238
AB  - Tracheal resection and primary anastomosis is the treatment of choice for a 
      short-segment stenosis. However, the procedure does carry the risk of two 
      potentially fatal complications: anastomosis breakdown and leak. We describe the 
      case of a 67-year-old man who was treated for a 3-cm tracheal stenosis secondary 
      to a prolonged intubation and multiple tracheostomies. The patient underwent a 
      tracheal resection and primary anastomosis. The anastomosis was reinforced with 
      fibrin sealant, which created an airtight seal. The patient was extubated 
      postoperatively, and he healed without complication. Fibrin sealant is a 
      convenient, safe, and effective material for reinforcing anastomotic suture 
      lines.
