TI  - Sedation in outpatient bronchoscopy.
PG  - 1145-53
AB  - Bronchoscopy is a procedure that is likely to provoke anxiety as the patient is 
      surrounded by monitoring and bronchoscopy equipment, and care is administered by 
      strangers who perform intimate, invasive, and sometimes, painful procedures. 
      Sedation is needed, therefore, to allay anxiety and reduce stress, improve 
      patient comfort and co-operation, provide amnesia and facilitate the 
      bronchoscopic procedure. In this review we try to summarize the current knowledge 
      on currently used sedation protocols with special reference to the commonly used 
      pharmacological agents. We believe sedation should be used routinely in 
      fiberoptic bronchoscopy in order to achieve a safe and pleasant procedure for 
      both the patient and the pulmonologist.
