TI  - Treatment of lobar atelectasis with bronchoscopically administered recombinant 
      human deoxyribonuclease in cystic fibrosis?
PG  - 123-6
LID - 10.1111/j.1752-699X.2007.00024.x [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Recombinant human deoxyribonuclease (rhDNase) reduces sputum 
      viscosity and improves pulmonary function in cystic fibrosis (CF). OBJECTIVE: The 
      objective of this study was to describe our experience in which rhDNase 
      (Pulmozyme; Roche, Basel, Switzerland) was administered by bronchoscopic 
      instillation into atelectatic lobes in five adults with CF. CONCLUSION: We found 
      this method successful in treating lobar atelectasis, which was resistant to 
      conventional therapy with antibiotics and physiotherapy. In all but one of the 
      cases we described, administration of DNase in this manner resulted in a 
      radiographic and clinical improvement of the atelectasis. We recommend that 
      respiratory physicians consider this as a second line treatment in the management 
      of atelectasis.
