TI  - [Perioperative management of airway foreign bodies in 35 pediatric patients].
PG  - 1065-70
AB  - We retrospectively reviewed 33 patients (35 cases) who underwent foreign body 
      removal at our institution from 1995 through 2003. Male-female ratio was 21 : 12 
      and the most frequent age was one year. The most common foreign bodies (FBs) were 
      nuts (n = 14) and plastics (n = 7). A repeater (3 cases) had mental retardation. 
      Patients were referred to our institution with an average interval of 90 hours, 
      and after 1.3 hospitals. All the patients were managed with general anesthesia. 
      Direct laryngoscopy was performed to extract FBs in 11 cases suspected of having 
      pharyngeal or laryngeal FBs. In 24 cases suspected of having tracheobronchial 
      FBs, the trachea was intubated and a flexible fiberoptic bronchoscopy was 
      performed to locate the FBs. FBs were found in the trachea in 2 cases and in the 
      bronchus in 18 patients and were successfully extracted by rigid bronchoscopy in 
      10 cases. All the patients were admitted for fear of laryngotracheal edema. There 
      were no significant postoperative complications.
