TI  - Primary pulmonary lymphoma disguised as asthma.
PG  - 127-9
AB  - Primary pulmonary lymphomas (PPL) only constitute 4% of extra-nodal, non-Hodgkin 
      lymphomas (NHL), less than 1% of NHL in general, and between 0.5% and 1% of 
      malignant pulmonary neoplasms. Fifty-eight to 87% of cases of this extremely 
      uncommon disease are low-grade B-cell lymphomas and 11%-19% are high-grade or 
      large B-cell lymphomas. The prognosis for.high-grade or large B-cell lymphomas is 
      worse than for low-grade lymphomas; respiratory and general symptoms are usually 
      present. However asthmatic symptoms are not often found in their clinic. We 
      report the case of a 49-year old woman with resistant asthma clinic in the form 
      of wheezing, dyspnea and non-productive painless cough; associated with an 
      irregular lobulated mass with air bronchograms in the lower lobe of the right 
      lung. Histological diagnosis showed a pattern of high-grade B-cell lymphoma and 
      all asthmatic symptoms disappeared following gross total resection of this 
      lesion.
