DP  - 2006 Jan
TI  - Subglottic stenosis in short-statured children: a case for further investigation 
      of airway symptoms in patients with skeletal dysplasias.
PG  - 147-53
AB  - Clinical evaluation of children with skeletal dysplasias is often concentrated on 
      morphologic and radiographic assessments, but many of these patients also have 
      disease processes of the ear, nose, and throat. We report a case of an 
      11-month-old girl with an unknown short-limbed dwarfism, similar to acromicric 
      dysplasia, with grade II subglottic stenosis. Laryngotracheoplasty with anterior 
      autologous costal cartilage graft and posterior cricoid split was performed at 
      age 13 months, with subsequent improvement of her airway status. In cases of 
      children with skeletal dysplasias and obstructive airway symptoms, formal 
      otolaryngologic evaluation is warranted for definitive diagnosis and treatment.
