DP  - 2004 Dec
TI  - A Christmas tree in the larynx.
PG  - 1016-20
AB  - A 2 year-old boy presented with acute upper airway obstruction following a 
      15-month history of noisy breathing and hoarseness. An urgent laryngotracheal 
      bronchoscopy was performed following inhalational induction of anesthesia. Using 
      a fiberoptic bronchoscope, visualization of the larynx through a laryngeal mask 
      airway revealed a flat plastic Christmas tree embedded within granulomatous cords 
      causing almost complete obstruction and requiring tracheostomy prior to 
      extraction. Twelve days later, the tracheostomy was successfully decannulated 
      with the child's voice beginning to normalize. The family remembered the 
      decoration from Christmas celebrations 2 years prior and recalled a coughing 
      episode that predated the onset of hoarseness.
