DP  - 2004 May
TI  - Diagnosis and treatment of traumatic intrathoracic major bronchial disruption.
PG  - 494-9
AB  - The purpose of this study was to review our experience with intrathoracic major 
      bronchial disruptions due to trauma and to describe the diagnosis and treatment. 
      METHODS: A retrospective review was performed of major bronchial injuries managed 
      in Kuwait (1995-2001), n=12. Clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, 
      surgical management, and outcome were reviewed. RESULTS: The mechanism of injury 
      involved blunt trauma in seven patients and penetrating trauma in five. Tachypnea 
      and subcutaneous emphysema (occurring in 10, 9 of the patients, respectively) are 
      common. Nine patients had pneumothorax and air leak after tube thoracostomy. The 
      diagnosis was confirmed by bronchoscopy in all patients. The majority of the 
      injuries were repaired primarily. Lung resection was necessary in two patients. 
      Four patients sustained complications including death (two patients), atelectasis 
      (one patient), and pneumonia (one patient). Follow-up bronchoscopy revealed no 
      evidence of granulation tissue or stenosis at the site of repair. CONCLUSION: The 
      diagnosis of intrathoracic major bronchial disruptions required a high index of 
      suspicion and liberal use of bronchoscopy. The majority were repaired primarily 
      with good outcome.
