VI  - 111
IP  - 3 Pt 1
DP  - 2002 Mar
TI  - Membranous tracheal rupture in children following minor blunt cervical trauma.
PG  - 197-9
AB  - Injuries to the tracheobronchial tree are well-recognized sequelae of massive 
      blunt or penetrating injuries of the neck or chest. They may also occur as a rare 
      complication of endotracheal intubation. We present 2 cases of a less 
      well-recognized clinical entity, rupture of the membranous trachea following 
      minimal blunt trauma to the neck in children. The case histories and management 
      of this disorder are discussed. Recognition and treatment of this problem 
      requires a high index of suspicion for the lesion and timely investigations. Open 
      repair of the trachea to secure a stable airway is recommended for this injury, 
      unless the wound is small and the wound edges are well approximated.
