VI  - 50
IP  - 1
DP  - 2002 Feb
TI  - Coexistence of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma and non-small cell lung carcinoma: 
      diagnosis and treatment.
PG  - 59-61
AB  - Abstract. In this communication, we will present a very rare case of the 
      coexistence of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL; low malignant lymphocytic lymphoma of 
      the B-cell type) and a non-small-cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC). A patient with a 
      15-year history of NHL developed a generalized relapse of the lymphoma with an 
      additional tumor mass in the left lower lobe of the lung. Bronchoscopy showed the 
      evidence of the NHL. Due to non-responding chemotherapy on the lung tumor, the 
      coexistence of a second malignancy was histologically proved in a second 
      bronchoscopy. Resection of the lung tumor with complex lobectomy and 
      lymphadenectomy was performed. After that, chemotherapy with four cycles of 
      carboplatin supplemented with taxol was induced. The patient was discharged from 
      the hospital with a stable remission of both tumor diseases. Restaging after six 
      months showed no evidence of a tumor relapse. This is a very rare case of the 
      coexistence of NHL and NSCLC; we will discuss the difficulty of diagnostic and 
      treatment of both tumor diseases.
