TI  - Left principal bronchus rupture in a cat.
PG  - 495-8
AB  - A two-year-old neutered male domestic shorthaired cat was referred with 
      respiratory compromise. Investigations included thoracic radiography and tracheal 
      endoscopy. Findings were consistent with a traumatic avulsion injury to the left 
      principal bronchus. Treatment involved the resection of the ruptured and stenosed 
      left principal bronchus segment via a fifth right intercostal lateral 
      thoracotomy. Once the damaged portion of bronchus had been removed, bronchial 
      repair was achieved by end-to-end anastomosis. A full recovery was made and, at 
      the time of writing (16 months postoperatively), the cat was clinically normal.
