VI  - 7 Suppl 1
DP  - 2000 Mar
TI  - [Role of endoscopy in the diagnosis of respiratory distress].
PG  - 10S-13S
AB  - In acute respiratory distress, bronchial endoscopy is useful to determine the 
      nature of dyspnea: inspiratory dyspnea in laryngeal abnormalities and 
      obstructions; mixed inspiratory and expiratory dyspnea in extrinsic and intrinsic 
      compressions of the tracheobronchial tree; overall, foreign bodies. Moreover, it 
      allows the evaluation of the lesions in bronchial contusions, tracheobronchial 
      burns and hemoptysis. Flexible (fiberoptic) and rigid endoscopy are complementary 
      methods. Bronchoalveolar lavage is useful in the microbiological investigation of 
      pneumonias developing in immuno compromised children.
